{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1516645-mage-game-styles","id":1516645,"title":"Mage Game Styles","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-06-29T07:31:32-07:00","id":1516645,"title":"Mage Game Styles","content":"I had some thoughts about different styles of play for Mage that I thought I'd share. Feel free to add your own.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">Full Pogrom</span></span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">This is the classic brutal Ascension War. The Technocracy is in ascendancy and the Traditions in hiding. Those mages with public personas had better be very careful because a single misstep could mean a luxury vacation to Room 101.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">This is the style I think of for early Mage when the Technocracy was The Man and soulless modernity and an omnipresent conspiracy all rolled into one. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><b>Partial Pogrom</b></span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">The Technocracy is brutal, but projects much more power than it actually has. They do not have the resources to crush every Reality Deviant, so they crush the most visible and the most dangerous. They still largely control the world, but they do so by being smart and calculating.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">This is the base style I think of when I think of Mage and I suspect I am not alone in that.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">Cells at Work</span></span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">The Technocracy and Traditions, as deep conspiracies, are largely independent cells. They have their own goals and missions and they do not interact with each other. They are mostly constrained by local resources and will only occasionally receive outside help unless things really get out of hand. The Men in Black aren\u2019t going to come to the rescue of the local Progenitor Lab unless things spill out into the open, but don\u2019t expect the Council to send any backup either.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">In this kind of game cabals and cohorts are expected to take care of themselves without much outside help. There\u2019s not a lot of backup to be had but there\u2019s also not much backup for the antagonists. If you do manage to take down the local Progenitor Lab, its going to be some time before anyone even notices. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><b>The Pax Coincidentia (aka Cold Ascension War)</b></span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">The Ascension War burns at a low simmer rather than a boil. An uneasy d\u00e9tente exists and everyone is willing to overlook a lot to make sure it stays that way. The fight continues, but civilly and coincidentally. No bullets, bombs, or disappearances in the night (unless you are sure you can get away with it). Instead, the war is fought with influence, propaganda, red tape, and petty one-upsmanship.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">This is a game where mages are very visible, but magic is not. Whether for political or metaphysical reasons, Vulgarity is very dangerous. Coincidental is the name of the game. </span></span></span><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">This scenario makes the most sense if, like the real Cold War, there is some existential threat like Mutually Assured Destruction that pushes everyone to keep the war cold. Things are stable, but one wrong step and they may not be for long.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">This one comes from an image I saw recently of two sides of an occult store with a Subway sandwich shop jammed (sandwiched, <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />) in between. It made me think of a game where that is the default move in the Ascension War.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">Where the Wild Things Are</span></span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">The Ascension War is only one of multiple problems the Traditions have to deal with. The Technocracy, by and large, controls the physical world but that\u2019s only a small part of reality. Out in the rest of the Tellurian there are gods and monsters, aliens and rogue mages, apocalypses and existential threats. Someone has to deal with them and the Technocracy has too many blind spots to be relied on.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">This style turns the Ascension War on its head. Now it is the Traditions who have to split their time between fighting the Ascension War and facing other threats to Earth. Humanity and the consensus are important but they are only one important thing among several.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">This one works especially well if it is the Traditions are firmly rooted in various Mythic Threads.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">Occupied Earth</span></span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">A combination of the previous two scenarios. The Technocracy takes the role of a hostile yet passive peacekeeping or occupying force. They still are trying to impose their paradigm on the world, but they think the job is mostly done and they are mostly maintaining the status quo at this point. There\u2019s a lot more magic left than they think, though, and it is up to the Traditions to help it grow while keeping it from spilling into the open. </span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">Reality on the Brink Factor: The Technocracy isn\u2019t nearly as victorious as they think. Strangeness keeps seeping through and its often not clear how dangerous it really is.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">In the Shadow of Angry Titans</span></span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">The Ascension War is largely a fight between Masters and Archmasters; veritable gods who have lost sight of the humanity they claim to fight for. In the trenches things are much messier and the Traditions and Technocracy rarely get to choose who they share a foxhole with when reality is bombarded. For those on earth, the name of the game is surviving the fallout, protecting the sleepers, and patching things back together. The players are just regular mages trying to survive a world that occasionally goes mad and where affiliation isn\u2019t as important as picking up the pieces.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">For a good idea of the feel of this one, think Mage the Awakening, but instead of the Tyranny of the Exarchs it is a raging Ascension War that oppresses Earth from the heavens. </span></span></span><br /> \u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-05T17:09:36-07:00","id":1517234,"title":"","content":"While &quot;The Pax Coincidentia&quot; is likely the ground state of Mage post-Avatar Storm, at least until whatever inciting incident threatens to push things into hot war &quot;now&quot;, I think &quot;Cells at Work&quot; is also an interesting angle to explore.<br /> <br /> It wasn't just the Traditions that had their Masters and Horizon Realms cut off. The Technocracy <i>literally</i> lost Control. The administrative threads that bound the whole Union together were severed. In effect, when the Technocracy detonated a ghost nuke in the Shadowlands, they inadvertently decapitated their own command structure.<br /> <br /> Moreover, it's likely most of the Union didn't even know that's what happened. Why would they? It's not like Control shares its activities with every operative on the ground. As far as most of them knew, Control suddenly and seemingly permanently went completely radio silent.<br /> <br /> Especially in the early days following the Avatar Storm, a great number of Constructs were effectively on their own. All they had was what connections they possessed on an individual basis. Nor could they necessarily trust one another. As I said, they didn't know what happened to Control. For all they knew, there was some strike that disrupted Control or killed it outright. Either from without (Traditions? Nephandi? Marauders? Something else?)...or within (The Friends of Courage? The SPD? Project Invictus? The so-called &quot;Panopticon&quot;?).<br /> <br /> If you're a Construct, left to flap in the wind without direction, who do you trust? What if the other Constructs demanding your agents and resources is part of the plot to take over the Union? What if the agents insisting you all need to stay together are in on it? What if your remaining superiors left earth-side are?<br /> <br /> All this not even getting into various factions - both the Conventions and those within or across them - that would use the destabilization of the Union for their own ends. Even if there was no foul play intended with the Avatar Storm, that doesn't mean there wouldn't be legitimate attempts to seize power or form coalitions.<br /> <br /> Of course, between the Traditions (who are experienced working as independent cells) and other Reality Deviants, a Construct can't afford to start openly pointing fingers or declaring war on other Constructs. Even if you ignored the Conditioning that makes them WANT to maintain the Union. So while the paranoia is palpable in the extreme, it's kept under control. Delicate dances occur, where Constructs and individual agents act loyal enough to avoid being censured, but not committing too much. No one wants to be caught working with a faction that turns out to have orchestrated the severing of Control, in case Control ever reestablishes itself. (Nor, indeed, is anyone eager to agree too readily to anyone <i>claiming</i> to be Control).<br /> <br /> If nothing else, the Union's scattered &quot;assets&quot; all agree, more or less unanimously, that they cannot <i>appear</i> fractured in front of other Mage factions. So when some Constructs start insisting, loudly and vociferously, that the Technocratic Union has &quot;won the Ascension War&quot;, no others will jump to refute that claim. They resent that words have been put in their mouths, but they don't deny it. They go along with the lie, to maintain appearances. Let the Reality Deviants think the Technocracy has pulled off some master stroke, and is now more powerful than ever.<br /> <br /> It's a bluff, and circumstances may conspire to call it. If Constructs are attacked - wiped out, even - can other Constructs afford to let it go unpunished? What if the Reality Deviants begin to see through the deceit, and see how precarious the whole Technocracy really is? Of course, committing too many resources in defense of other Constructs is itself a gamble. Without a centralized supply network, can a Construct afford to stretch itself thin? And what if they people asking for aid...are &quot;in on it&quot;? What if it's a ploy?<br /> <br /> Who can you trust?<br /> <br /> Some Constructs may even think working with the Traditions or Marauders (<b>never</b> the Nephandi, though, ofc), over their own colleagues is a more sound move. At least you can trust a Reality Deviant to be a deviant. They often wear their intent on their sleeves. A fellow Technocrat from outside your circle of personally vetted assets is much less easy to pin down. You would know, since you ARE one.<br /> <br /> All this in service of rebuilding what was lost. Investigating the truth. Sussing out other Technocrats, to see who can (nominally) be trusted. It takes a long time. A Construct can ill afford to pick unnecessary fights. Only the most overt instances of &quot;Reality Terrorism&quot; warrant attention, because that's all the Union can deal with. Not until and unless Control can be reestablished. One way or another.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":6156,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","user":"Bluecho","date":". 07-05-2023, 09:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-09T17:51:30-07:00","id":1541822,"title":"","content":"Necroing this post, as it has everything to do with my current chronicle.<br /> <br /> I'm actually going for another approach entirely: the Ascension War is over. Not one of the sides, but the war itself.<br /> <br /> For now it's just the beginning of it, but little by little both sides are noticing that the effort simply isn't feasible anymore, neither their old goals make sense in the current world.<br /> <br /> Part of it is that, in a sense, both factions lost. The modern world doesn't play by their books, most people believe in a plethora of things, and this essentially went out of control.<br /> <br /> This partially plays in favor of the Traditions, but without their masters and with limited influence, it's a &quot;victory&quot; with little actual benefit for them.<br /> <br /> Secretly the faction that's really winning are the Nefandi. The rise of the Far Right around the world is their biggest victory. They're finally in a position in which they can ignore how most people are better informed, and just be openly evil, hateful and destructive.<br /> <br /> Both the Traditions and the Union must find new purposes in this new reality, which is slowly drawing new political lines within and between them, while the Nefandi become an ever more pressing threat.<br /> <br /> Also, this Nefandus victory is slowly disintegrating the Consensus as a prequel to the disintegration of reality. So Paradox is back to 2nd Edition levels and improving, Reality Zones are more common, and overall the dangerous part of the Mythic Age is coming back.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-10T09:49:12-07:00","id":1541873,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1516645-mage-game-styles?p=1541822#post1541822\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Necroing this post, as it has everything to do with my current chronicle.<br /> <br /> I'm actually going for another approach entirely: the Ascension War is over. Not one of the sides, but the war itself.<br /> <br /> For now it's just the beginning of it, but little by little both sides are noticing that the effort simply isn't feasible anymore, neither their old goals make sense in the current world.<br /> <br /> Part of it is that, in a sense, both factions lost. The modern world doesn't play by their books, most people believe in a plethora of things, and this essentially went out of control.<br /> <br /> This partially plays in favor of the Traditions, but without their masters and with limited influence, it's a &quot;victory&quot; with little actual benefit for them.<br /> <br /> Secretly the faction that's really winning are the Nefandi. The rise of the Far Right around the world is their biggest victory. They're finally in a position in which they can ignore how most people are better informed, and just be openly evil, hateful and destructive.<br /> <br /> Both the Traditions and the Union must find new purposes in this new reality, which is slowly drawing new political lines within and between them, while the Nefandi become an ever more pressing threat.<br /> <br /> Also, this Nefandus victory is slowly disintegrating the Consensus as a prequel to the disintegration of reality. So Paradox is back to 2nd Edition levels and improving, Reality Zones are more common, and overall the dangerous part of the Mythic Age is coming back.</div> </div> </div> </div>More like that the Technocracy has won a decisive blown and it is closer than ever to installing a 1984 world government of total dystopic control under the Far Left, where the leftist NPCs repeats everything that governments and multinational corporations say verbatim while still believing themselves to be &quot;the resistance&quot;.<br /> <br /> See how easy it is do play that game?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-10T11:54:17-07:00","id":1541878,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1516645-mage-game-styles?p=1541873#post1541873\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> More like that the Technocracy has won a decisive blown and it is closer than ever to installing a 1984 world government of total dystopic control under the Far Left, where the leftist NPCs repeats everything that governments and multinational corporations say verbatim while still believing themselves to be &quot;the resistance&quot;.<br /> <br /> See how easy it is do play that game?</div> </div> </div> </div>Seems delusional to me, but you do you.<br /> <br /> I'd appreciate input on the overall idea, though.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":2},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-10T12:56:38-07:00","id":1541881,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1516645-mage-game-styles?p=1541878#post1541878\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Seems delusional to me, but you do you.<br /> <br /> I'd appreciate input on the overall idea, though.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you want to use political themes in a game you should absolutely never introduce Nephandi or allude to their involvement. Actual human evil isn't the product of people who are ontologically evil, it's the product of a bunch of very nice people who are <i>also</i> bigots, or particularly greedy, or whatever. But most of them--especially the ones driving most of this--go home to their nice, loving families at the end of a day of doing horrible things. The head of Auschwitz loved his daughter. Ronald and Nancy Reagan clearly had a loving relationship. Woodrow Wilson was a vicious racist but also helped create the League of Nations.<br /> <br /> If you want to say that the rise of far-right elements is a problem for the Technocracy and Traditions, just have the enemies be the far-right without having them be Nephandic. Though you're gong to need to explain why this is a new problem, given that right-wing authoritiarism is not a new invention and that previous decades had panics over water fluoridation and the Scopes Monkey Trial... the other problem in having &quot;the Nephandi are winning&quot; as a plot point is that none of this is actually <i>new</i>. If you look back at what public discourse was like in the past, most of the problems attributed to the Nephandi gaining strength aren't actually new. Reagan blatantly lying about the Iran-Contra affair or Bush lying about Iraq having WMDs just isn't as salient for Americans as whatever Trump said in the past week.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-10T15:08:16-07:00","id":1541889,"title":"","content":"My idea is that the Nefandi are winning in a general sense, but not that they're directly involved with those people. It's less that they caused the Far Right and more that the Far Right is partially an effect of their success.<br /> <br /> I'll take some time to think about that, nonetheless, they're more something for my overarching idea for the setting than the current chronicle itself.<br /> <br /> And this is less a new problem and more an old problem neglected for too long, and becoming more evident as the Ascension War slows to a halt. Even then, a lot of people on both sides may not perceive it as such.<br /> <br /> Overall the point is that reality is weakening, which is a victory to the Nefandi by default, but their actual involvement is less relevant. Also the new lines forming to unite and divide the Awakened, which may either help them come together to figure out what's happening, or create divisions that blind them again.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-10T17:50:19-07:00","id":1541901,"title":"","content":"Why is reality weakening, then?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-11T16:55:17-07:00","id":1541939,"title":"","content":"Good question. I thought I had a good answer, but maybe not, one of those things that seemed to make perfect sense at some point, then poof, just can't remember.<br /> <br /> For this current chronicle I have an answer, but it was meant to be just an opportunity attack in the great scheme of things. A cabal of Marauders with an actual master plan to plunge reality in chaos.<br /> <br /> You really got me to rethink what I was doing, thank you very much for the feedback!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-13T01:35:19-07:00","id":1542035,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1516645-mage-game-styles?p=1541889#post1541889\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My idea is that the Nefandi are winning in a general sense, but not that they're directly involved with those people. It's less that they caused the Far Right and more that the Far Right is partially an effect of their success.<br /> <br /> I'll take some time to think about that, nonetheless, they're more something for my overarching idea for the setting than the current chronicle itself.<br /> <br /> And this is less a new problem and more an old problem neglected for too long, and becoming more evident as the Ascension War slows to a halt. Even then, a lot of people on both sides may not perceive it as such.<br /> <br /> Overall the point is that reality is weakening, which is a victory to the Nefandi by default, but their actual involvement is less relevant. Also the new lines forming to unite and divide the Awakened, which may either help them come together to figure out what's happening, or create divisions that blind them again.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> The problem with, and in general, with scenario trying to make modern politics relevant to mage, is that the consensus', and hence reality's stability is not dependant on politics. <br /> <br /> To win, the nephandi would need to push the beliefs of people toward the world ending in order to make it more possible to accomplish.<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> <br /> They can't weaken reality with raising the far right. Even the worst result wouldn't be different than what the setting was like in dark age page or sorcerer's crusade. <br /> <br /> We may think modern politics are relevant because we live them, but compared to most of history, nothing is really new under the sun. If xenophobic nations didn' t kill reality in the last 4 millenias there's no reasons it will start doing so now. <br /> <br /> Which IMHO is one of the problem of M20 nephandi. They don't actually do much. <br /> <br /> The only thing that may make it relevant is if it push technological research or alternative paradigms in the forefront. <br /> <br /> Something the traditions, amongs others, would benefit from. <br /> <br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> \u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24043-nyremne","name":"Nyremne","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-13T09:40:49-07:00","id":1542062,"title":"","content":"And even then, reality's stability isn't dependent on the stability of the Consensus. Or rather, it is and it isn't. It is in that if Consensus isn't stable you do get a lot of little reality zones and the rules aren't always consistent. But reality is still there. Its inhabitants thrive and are healthy. This form of instability isn't a bad thing. I'd even argue it's a <i>good</i> thing, since the Technocracy's stable Consensus has become so rock-solid they can't release new technologies to the Masses and there's a lot of beings and cultures that are excluded from Earth by the modern stable Consensus.<br /> <br /> You could run a game where the problem is Consensus itself, I suppose. Have the PCs find out that Consensus didn't use to exist. The reason that in prior eras, a lack of a steady Consensus didn't cause problems for reality was that human perceptions weren't warping reality. Then you could say the Technocracy has gradually lost control of it and that's threatening to rip reality in half, but you'd need to massively play up the degree to which they have lost control. For instance, anti-vaxxer sentiment shouldn't do that because anti-vaxxers still believe in the fundamentals of the Technocratic paradigm--they got their start because of a fraudulent scientific study and they still believe in the fundamentals of the Technocratic paradigm. Instead you'd have to have them reject germ theory and embrace humorism.<br /> <br /> The PCs have to figure this out, then decide if they're going to try and fight over Consensus or just try and abolish it entirely.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-13T12:31:49-07:00","id":1542070,"title":"","content":"It seems to me that the one possible impasse is <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar5826_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5826\" data-vbnamecard=\"5826\">monteparnas</a> is attempting to portray in their chronicle two things happening simultaneously. Consensus is calcifying and growing stronger while &quot;reality&quot; is weakening. This has some parallels with the &quot;Consensus of Apathy&quot; introduced in M:Rev. At the time I had some reservations of that concept. It came with mechanical adjustments to casting and paradox I wasn't used to as well as a bevy of other mechanical or plot changes such as the Avatar Storm which, I feel, narrowed rather than expanded the setting.<br /> <br /> Let me set that distaste aside for a while. Decoupled from the mechanical changes the Consensus of Apathy is actually quite a good setting option. Its very on theme for the World of Darkness. Raging against apathy is very punk. So how do we integrate the Consensus of Apathy into their world to get the result they are going for?<br /> <br /> Im going to offer what I call the &quot;Material Science&quot; solution: <i>Disclaimer- I am not a scientist just a fan of science. </i>Hardness measures how resistant a material is to surface deformation. Although the correlation isn't universal hardness often comes at the price brittleness. As the technocratic consensus hardens it also becomes less able to bend and more brittle. On a local level maybe this means any successful and sufficiently vulgar magic could cause actual damage to the Technocratic reality zone, weakening or destroying it. Or maybe cracks and holes propagate both in geography and time. Maybe at one point all of New York was safely a Technocratic reality zone...but lately jewish mysticism is easier to perform in Midwood (mostly orthodox Jewish population) and vodou in Flatbush (large afro-Haitian population). Maybe for 20 hours a day downtown Wall Street is a technocratic paradise...but after hours in the clubs when the music is pumping and the drugs are flowing an Ecstatic can get away with a lot more.<br /> <br /> If I remember correctly something similar happens towards the end of the <i>Ascension </i>novel. Reality starts breaking down, local paradigms flourish, magic gets easier to perform, awakenings skyrocket, and eventually the gauntlet breaks down and the Tellurian as a whole rushes towards unity and oneness. Making this a story of Nephandic <i>Descension </i>would require some changes, of course. At the start, or even for most of the Chronicles the characters may view the new reality zones as an unmistakable good. But perhaps it is Nephandic magic (not vulgar magic) that disrupts Technocratic reality zones, making the flourishing of differing zones a poisoned chalice. This could all be happening because the TU is otherwise distracted with their own concerns (TU civil war, threat null) and it may require working with them (or some of their less murderhobo-y factions) to correct.<br /> <br /> At the risk of extending the metaphor too far - What is one of the best ways to strengthen a metallic material with a lot of hardness but too much brittleness? Its alloying. Mixing in a more flexible metal that is able to absorb and redirect energy and prevent immediate fracture. The TU allying (alloying) with the Traditions may be the only way to preserve the consensus but good luck convincing either side of that.<br /> <br /> I had another model in my head called the &quot;Tectonic Plates&quot; model. It involved reality zones shifting, moving, bumping up against one another and occasionally popping up unexpected places (&quot;Why is my Apalachin woods witch magic so easy in downtown Shanghai?&quot;). The origin of the problem is pretty much the same with slightly different results. I'd love to expand more on this but unfortunately I went to lunch in the middle of writing this and now desperately need to appear busy at work.<br /> <br /> Oh, and not that you need ME to tell you this <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar5826_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5826\" data-vbnamecard=\"5826\">monteparnas</a> but keep politics in your game. Make your game about a bunch of unhoused AuDHD queerdos fighting a demonically possessed Steve Bannon if that's what you want. If you want to make the alt-right nephandic than make them nephandic. The fact that &quot;things have been bad before&quot; is in no way a useful response to the fact that things are bad NOW. In fact, that position is the Consensus of Apathy in action. The reason why the struggle matters here and now is because here and now is where we currently are. Of course the alt-right isn't powered by literal metaphysical evil in our world. But in your game, at your table, it can be so. Maybe because it gives you a sense of control and agency in the world just for length of one gaming session. Maybe because you think its funny. Doesn't matter. It's your table, your game, your world, your rules.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6350-marcusvitel","name":"MarcusVitel","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"20"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-13T15:12:44-07:00","id":1542074,"title":"","content":"Yeah, the consensus of apathy was always a terrible idea. Of course for it's existence purely to restrict the scope of the game, but more so because it goes against the basic premises of the game. <br /> <br /> Your idea that the technocracy solidified the consensus too much <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6350-marcusvitel\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"6350\" data-vbnamecard=\"6350\">MarcusVitel</a> would make sense in it's place. But the worse part of the concept of the consensus of apathy is that it assume that apathy is relevant to the consensus. <br /> <br /> The consensus is not &quot;I feel so and so about X&quot; it's &quot;I believe x is possible&quot;. <br /> <br /> So if I cast a fireball near a sleeper while explaining the whole thing to him and steeling myself for the incoming paradox, it doesn't matter if he goes &quot;whatever&quot;, as long as he accept that fireball throwing people exist, he influence the consensus a iota in my direction. <br /> <br /> I have this theory that somewhen in the development of the wod, people writing on MtA also wrote on changeling. And they kept forgetting which metaphysics belongs to which. And that would be why we have so much nonsense on apathy in Mrev or the importance of the rise of hatred in M20, and on the other side more and more mention in belief in Ctd. <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24043-nyremne","name":"Nyremne","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-13T16:36:52-07:00","id":1542079,"title":"","content":"The thing with apathy is actually present in every WoD game line since the beginning, although MRev did got it to a whole new level. But it is a 90's thing, specially in the US but not only in the US. It was the End of History sentiment.<br /> <br /> But as Phil Brucatto says in the Book of Secrets, the ideas that came with this sentiment were completely wrong. Magic was pretty much alive, and still is. Most people have some sort of magical thinking and almost everyone has at least some magical beliefs. In fact, even skeptics are mostly people that learn to set their beliefs aside, not people who don't believe in some weird shit.<br /> <br /> And because of that, my first idea is that the Consensus of Apathy, and maybe the Consensus itself, is someone else's doing. But it is breaking. The current world is far different in this regard from the apathetic future the devs thought were coming, and so I think in Mage that means the Consensus is weakening.<br /> <br /> On itself this is not Reality breaking, just the Consensus, as <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar28383_1.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"28383\" data-vbnamecard=\"28383\">Citrakayah</a> said. The Union thought it had power over the beliefs of the general population and they were making the Consensus, but while they got a lot of power and a lot of influence over its course, ultimately they were wrong, and today's world is proof of this, and it has nothing to do with the Nefandi (unless they're the ones to actually create the Consensus, maybe as a means to 'make Reality brittle').<br /> <br /> Now, the thing about the far right isn't about Consensus, or about how numerous they are, but about more pressing matters. We still live in a world kind of on the brink of destruction. While millenarianism receded, the actual real problems that threat our future remain: climate change went from a looming threat in the 90's to actual reality and worsening, nations with WMDs are ever more belligerent, we just recently proved we can't unite against an epidemic even having all the tools to control it.<br /> <br /> Here's where the far-right comes. They're openly positioning against everything we can do to make this mess a little less shitty. This is not by any stretch humans being worse than we've been through history, but it is the consequences of their evil becoming far greater than before. In this situation, the Nefandi don't need to work the Consensus, just work to keep bad people in a place of enough power to stop any meaningful change (and I definitely agree that bad people shouldn't be bad just because of supernatural influence, and shouldn't think themselves bad at all, quite the opposite).<br /> <br /> Oh, and just to be clear: my whole Mage group is made up of left-wing people. Real left-wing, not the progressive right-wing of the US Democrats.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":5826,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","user":"monteparnas","date":". 09-13-2024, 07:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-13T18:21:23-07:00","id":1542081,"title":"","content":"Thinking more on this, Consensus as an evil plot (Nephandic or otherwise) has a lot going for it.<br /> <br /> 1. If you believe that reality is entirely determined by Consensus, you don't really need to do the hard work of changing things if you can just convince people it's not necessarily. Why change the world to solve global warming if you can just deceive people into thinking global warming doesn't exist? That should get rid of the problem, right? You should be able to do the same for environmental problems generally; just have people stick their fingers in their ear and not listen and disbelieve it out of existence. Even many social problems should function the same way.<br /> 2. We know there are alternate routes to magic and expanding the human technological base. It's less versatile and less powerful, but sorcerers can create new paths without any involvement from mages or any Mythic Threads (see the Starlight path, which has no real world folkloric basis). Yet mage factions are almost exclusively focused on Consensus and Sphere magic. There's a lot of other things that don't seem to be affected by Consensus and some logical issues posed by its existence that would be neatly explained if it didn't always exist and wasn't always as strong.<br /> 3. From a meta perspective, Time of Judgment scenarios involving Nephandi have emphasized that there are no simple answers and anything that appears to be a simple answer is probably a trap. Changing people's minds is hard. That's politics for you. But &quot;If we all agree, we can will utopia into existence&quot; is still a lot simpler than the grunt work of actually building a better society from the ground up. And in this hypothetical, the Nephandi <i>aren't making people do anything with Consensus</i>. They just created it and let mages fight over it. This ties into the theme of hubris and rivalries dooming the world.<br /> 4. Consensus is <i>really</i> good at genocide. There's the cultural genocide of making entire culture's society no longer function--imagine being a mythical society that calls on the gods to bless your fields and then suddenly that starts failing--but it's taken parts of the globe away from Earth and into the Umbra. And it will outright kill Bygones. How many sapient species did we use to share the planet with who are all dead now because they were murdered by disbelief? Mage often posits Consensus as this egalitarian liberating thing that allows the human species to decide its own destiny... and maybe this is just me being a Werewolf main, but giving one species control over that always seemed like the least egalitarian thing imaginable. Even if we're just concerned with humans, Consensus is a perfect weapon against society's minorities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-13T22:00:21-07:00","id":1542094,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1516645-mage-game-styles?p=1542079#post1542079\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The thing with apathy is actually present in every WoD game line since the beginning, although MRev did got it to a whole new level. But it is a 90's thing, specially in the US but not only in the US. It was the End of History sentiment.<br /> <br /> But as Phil Brucatto says in the Book of Secrets, the ideas that came with this sentiment were completely wrong. Magic was pretty much alive, and still is. Most people have some sort of magical thinking and almost everyone has at least some magical beliefs. In fact, even skeptics are mostly people that learn to set their beliefs aside, not people who don't believe in some weird shit.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>To be fair, brucato wasn't really mentioning the consensus of apathy in his addendum of the BoS. <br /> <br /> He was speaking of the idea permeating MtA that people no longer believe in magic. <br /> <br /> The consensus of apathy being specifically a Mrev thing. And the problem is not helped by it's omnipresence in the wod. There are many things that are present in either one game or most of them that doesn't make sense in another. <br /> <br /> Apathy may be important in wta or ctd, but not in MtA. Only beliefs really matters there. <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24043-nyremne","name":"Nyremne","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-13T22:09:07-07:00","id":1542095,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1516645-mage-game-styles?p=1542081#post1542081\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thinking more on this, Consensus as an evil plot (Nephandic or otherwise) has a lot going for it.<br /> <br /> 1. If you believe that reality is entirely determined by Consensus, you don't really need to do the hard work of changing things if you can just convince people it's not necessarily. Why change the world to solve global warming if you can just deceive people into thinking global warming doesn't exist? That should get rid of the problem, right? You should be able to do the same for environmental problems generally; just have people stick their fingers in their ear and not listen and disbelieve it out of existence. Even many social problems should function the same way.<br /> 2. We know there are alternate routes to magic and expanding the human technological base. It's less versatile and less powerful, but sorcerers can create new paths without any involvement from mages or any Mythic Threads (see the Starlight path, which has no real world folkloric basis). Yet mage factions are almost exclusively focused on Consensus and Sphere magic. There's a lot of other things that don't seem to be affected by Consensus and some logical issues posed by its existence that would be neatly explained if it didn't always exist and wasn't always as strong.<br /> 3. From a meta perspective, Time of Judgment scenarios involving Nephandi have emphasized that there are no simple answers and anything that appears to be a simple answer is probably a trap. Changing people's minds is hard. That's politics for you. But &quot;If we all agree, we can will utopia into existence&quot; is still a lot simpler than the grunt work of actually building a better society from the ground up. And in this hypothetical, the Nephandi <i>aren't making people do anything with Consensus</i>. They just created it and let mages fight over it. This ties into the theme of hubris and rivalries dooming the world.<br /> 4. Consensus is <i>really</i> good at genocide. There's the cultural genocide of making entire culture's society no longer function--imagine being a mythical society that calls on the gods to bless your fields and then suddenly that starts failing--but it's taken parts of the globe away from Earth and into the Umbra. And it will outright kill Bygones. How many sapient species did we use to share the planet with who are all dead now because they were murdered by disbelief? Mage often posits Consensus as this egalitarian liberating thing that allows the human species to decide its own destiny... and maybe this is just me being a Werewolf main, but giving one species control over that always seemed like the least egalitarian thing imaginable. Even if we're just concerned with humans, Consensus is a perfect weapon against society's minorities.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> You rise good points. But the thing is, these are simply things that are problems because writers didn't really though about the logical implication of the metaphysics of the game.<br /> <br /> 1/ Yes, logically the technocracy should encourage climate change skepticism to either reduce the problem or eliminate it. Or at least claim it's a nothing burger that will be resolved with new shiny tech. And on the contrary, nephandi should encourage climate activism to spread the belief that climate change may end the world, hence making it more likely.<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b2/ another thing missed by the devs. It's indeed a conversation I had with basically every group knowing about the setting.<br /> Raw, the ascension war would be dealt with in a few years span. Sent the sorcerers in public, make them use magic, they can't suffer paradox.<br /> <br /> The existence of sorcerers is a death blow to the logic of the ascension war as currently written<br /> <br /> 3/ to be fair, changing minds in mage is not that hard. Find people that groups trusts, and use the mind sphere to convince them of something, then they'll spread the word<br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b4/it's actually worse than you think.<br /> Yes, entire populations of sapient bygones were wiped out by disbelief (and the void seekers casting them out of the map to the umbra), but that also happen out world.<br /> It's pretty clear that civilisations existed/exist on other planets, and that the consensus that space is an empty void either killed. A lot of them or threw them to shade realms","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24043,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24043-nyremne","user":"Nyremne","date":". 09-14-2024, 04:33 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24043-nyremne","name":"Nyremne","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-14T10:40:23-07:00","id":1542120,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyremne</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1516645-mage-game-styles?p=1542094#post1542094\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> To be fair, brucato wasn't really mentioning the consensus of apathy in his addendum of the BoS.<br /> <br /> He was speaking of the idea permeating MtA that people no longer believe in magic.<br /> <br /> The consensus of apathy being specifically a Mrev thing. And the problem is not helped by it's omnipresence in the wod. There are many things that are present in either one game or most of them that doesn't make sense in another.<br /> <br /> Apathy may be important in wta or ctd, but not in MtA. Only beliefs really matters there.<br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Those are related concepts. WoD always rolled with the idea that people became apathetic with the world. This is clear since Vampire 1st Ed. People live meaningless lives, are desensitized to the problems of society, and don't believe &quot;for real&quot; in anything.<br /> <br /> It's Punk Literature 101, the protagonists dare to care in a world who doesn't.<br /> <br /> But all in all, the point is that it doesn't hold as they thought at the time, and didn't hold. More than that, it has all the problems you address. Consensus obviously didn't always work according to the theory, something was off.<br /> <br /> So yeah, it makes some sense that it was actually something very different from what the Union thought, and even them shouldn't be putting a lot of trust on it. It isn't the Will of the Masses, it isn't natural, and it isn't at all under their control.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-14T14:41:18-07:00","id":1542135,"title":"","content":"If I had to bring MtAs into the present, I'd probably iterate on the idea I had earlier for a post-Avatar Storm Technocracy being on the backfoot. While also using an idea I posited back in the thread about custom Crafts (which I sort of jumped off into specifically the &quot;Sons of Liberty&quot;).<br /> <br /> Namely that in the wake of the Avatar Storm and the literal loss of Control, the Technocracy lost the reins it had over global society. It's state apparatuses, its economic systems, its information networks, and its culture. The Technocracy never completely controlled any of these things, of course. But they could guide them. Use all manner of levers and tools to nudge our world in a direction of the Union's liking. And, importantly for our purposes, moderate forces that could throw their Neo-Liberal hegemony out of &quot;proper&quot; alignment.<br /> <br /> With the loss of Control, the systems put in place to direct society were broken. Their ties to every strata of the Capitalist system severed, their assets cut off and gone to seed. Knowledge of even what all those assets and systems were was lost. In much the way that &quot;client Crafts&quot; slipped the leash of the Union, so did its assets embedded in government, corporation, media, and the tech sector. As the Technocracy struggled to get its own house(s) in order, a process of several years, the global edifice they helped build kept running without them. Unconstrained, unguided, and unmoderated.<br /> <br /> Fascism, always a movement festering in the underbelly of Conservativism, was a beast leashed by Sleeper and Enlightened forces alike. Used as a solid base that could be manipulated through dog whistles and propaganda to maintain the Status Quo, and occasionally act as a bludgeon forces that would threaten the Neo-Liberal hegemony. Fascism is and always has been, after all, Capitalism's immune response against Socialism.<br /> <br /> Now, the Technocracy's ability to restrain both the worst excesses of Capitalism and the spread of Fascism were compromised. The beast they thought they could control slipped its tether, and started feasting. The Syndicate could not or didn't want to prevent Capitalism from exploiting people in new and creative ways (enabled by technology), beyond what it previously allowed. And Fascism preyed upon the growing discontent within people at their tightening economic bonds, providing no end of scapegoats for the problems directly caused by corporate greed and control.<br /> <br /> Moreover, in a time of increased social media dominance and consolidation, the Technocracy wasn't there to stop algorithms from blindly pointing users towards radicalizing content. YouTube offering via Autoplay all manner of videos decrying the scientific establishment and fomenting belief in conspiracy theories of an often supernatural bent. Something the Union would never have permitted, and might otherwise have prevented by simply using their agents embedded in YouTube to tweak the algorithms away from such content. Such tools were outside the Technocracy's reach, allowing reactionary movements to snowball through blind systems of profit optimization and engagement farming.<br /> <br /> The resulting reactionary movements - such as Gamergate - redpilled whole generations of people into not only believing unscientific, unsecular worldviews, but galvanized them into harassment campaigns and outright mass violence. (Nephandi weren't responsible for this, of course, but they could have empowered certain personalities or signal-boosted radicalizing messages further. Perhaps been in key places to nudge individuals into lone wolf violence, that in turn inspired more. But simply funneling money to right-wing grifters and ideologues is more than sufficient to allow a Fascist movement to snowball, even without dark magick.)<br /> <br /> All while corporate interests, unfettered even by the true believers among the Syndicate, pushed Capitalism harder. More deregulation, more propaganda, more disinformation, more exploitation. Billionaires became richer, more powerful, more deranged, and more immune to being affected by Union assets that remained (simply by virtue of their popular visibility; could the same forces that keep presidents and prime ministers from being influenced also shield figures like Elon Musk?). Instead being guided by their own greed, and the same mundane radicalizing forces that play on the common people. Technofetishists no longer seek to further actual innovation (sanctioned and scheduled by the Technocracy, of course), but by any grifters promising impossible technology at unrealistic rates of return. Enamored by thoughts of unlimited growth, greater control of consumers and workers, and the promise of leaving the dying planet behind for off-world colonies. Distractions from the very real problems they allowed to creep in, if not outright enabled to exist, especially Climate Change.<br /> <br /> In the end, what are we left with? Well, the rise of Fascist movements, the mass disillusionment of the populace in the promise of Neo-Liberal policies and systems, a growing leftist movement to foster collective power (and a blunt-force state crackdown on same, as the illusion of representation is eroded away to reveal a violent, repressive totalitarianism, <i>even among the liberal centrists</i>). And the mass rejection of Science and Progress, in favor of reactionary mysticism and religious fanaticism.<br /> <br /> The world of Mage in the modern day is one typified by the Technocracy struggling to regain the reins, and the Traditions struggling to fight against the hateful ideology worming its way into their movements.<br /> <br /> This must be what it felt like to be a Mage in 1930s Europe, with the PREVIOUS rise of Fascism. Except with more nukes, and no one in control.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-14T16:29:37-07:00","id":1542144,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1516645-mage-game-styles?p=1542120#post1542120\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Those are related concepts. WoD always rolled with the idea that people became apathetic with the world. This is clear since Vampire 1st Ed. People live meaningless lives, are desensitized to the problems of society, and don't believe &quot;for real&quot; in anything.<br /> <br /> It's Punk Literature 101, the protagonists dare to care in a world who doesn't.<br /> <br /> But all in all, the point is that it doesn't hold as they thought at the time, and didn't hold. More than that, it has all the problems you address. Consensus obviously didn't always work according to the theory, something was off.<br /> <br /> So yeah, it makes some sense that it was actually something very different from what the Union thought, and even them shouldn't be putting a lot of trust on it. It isn't the Will of the Masses, it isn't natural, and it isn't at all under their control.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The one problem I see with that is that the consensus is one of, if not THE, core element of mage. <br /> To take it away would rewrite the setting in a way that it would be something as different as, say, mtaw. <br /> <br /> Not to say it isn't possible, after all, dark age mage kept the issue in the dark. Howether if we were to built a world where the consensus is an aberration, how can it be replaced without making it look like a massive retcon, so to speak?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24043-nyremne","name":"Nyremne","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-14T16:43:32-07:00","id":1542146,"title":"","content":"To be specific, I think one the big issue of that is not so much the core setting, but what it became post 1st edition.<br /> <br /> Mafe begin with a paradox of difficult to reconcile concepts. &quot;a world like our own&quot; &quot;a world where reality is decided by consensus&quot; and &quot; a world where magic and the supernatural are hidden&quot;. <br /> <br /> These three contradict each others. How could the supernatural be hidden in a world where reality is decided by beliefs like those of our own? How could the world be like ours if reality bend to belief? How could humans accepts rationalism and ontological materialism as a society when when those ideas where formed, supernatural events would happen everywhere? <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b Why would people suffer from medical issues when the belief in the four humors should work as much as modern medicine back then? <br /> <br /> <br /> 1st edition was the most coherent on that. In fact I would say most issues of MtA is that 2nd edition was basically a massive reboot, changing most of the metaphysics while keeping most of the elements<br /> <br /> So in 1st Ed reality is absolutly fluid, any awakened fundamentally knows it. Paradigm is merely a tool, technocrats knows they are mages. And the only reason he world is as it is, is because the technocracy is such a powerful thing that it is basically the archons of a gnostic setting. <br /> <br /> So it makes sense in that world that we went from the beliefs of the past to one where magic is apparently absent. The overlords of reality tricked the world hardly. <br /> <br /> Now come 2nd Ed, where factions can't agree on the nature of reality, and the technocracy is as deluded as everyone else. <br /> <br /> <br /> The only way this setting can hold is if the tech ocracy rules absolutly. But the problem is that edition after edition, the technocracy is weaker and weaker. <br /> <br /> To the point that there's zero reason for why the world in m20 is anywhere close to our real world. <br /> <br /> <br /> Ironically forcing us DMs to houserule our own paradigm to justify this mess.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24043-nyremne","name":"Nyremne","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-14T17:06:41-07:00","id":1542148,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyremne</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1516645-mage-game-styles?p=1542144#post1542144\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> The one problem I see with that is that the consensus is one of, if not THE, core element of mage.<br /> To take it away would rewrite the setting in a way that it would be something as different as, say, mtaw.<br /> <br /> Not to say it isn't possible, after all, dark age mage kept the issue in the dark. Howether if we were to built a world where the consensus is an aberration, how can it be replaced without making it look like a massive retcon, so to speak?</div> </div> </div> </div>What I would suggest is having Consensus only come into existence between the Dark Ages and Victorian Age, and gradually strengthen over time. Mages really haven't been able to distinguish between that and Consensus solidifying due to the Technocracy's influence.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-14T17:58:52-07:00","id":1542150,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1516645-mage-game-styles?p=1542148#post1542148\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> What I would suggest is having Consensus only come into existence between the Dark Ages and Victorian Age, and gradually strengthen over time. Mages really haven't been able to distinguish between that and Consensus solidifying due to the Technocracy's influence.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> That may indeed work. This would means that the &quot;natural&quot; state of things on earth would be the world of dark ages, but without even traces of the scourge?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24043-nyremne","name":"Nyremne","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-14T18:26:52-07:00","id":1542154,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyremne</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1516645-mage-game-styles?p=1542150#post1542150\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> That may indeed work. This would means that the &quot;natural&quot; state of things on earth would be the world of dark ages, but without even traces of the scourge?</div> </div> </div> </div>Or the Scourge existed, but it worked differently from how the books portray it--maybe various supernatural entities would either bolster or suppress certain kinds of sphere magic, and over time accounts of that have gotten garbled into what modern mages think the Scourge was (there's not <i>that</i> many mages who were around during that period). That's still not Paradox or the Consensus because the sources is very different, but it could be difficult for an external observer to tell the difference between a Reality Zone generated by Consensus and a local god or powerful spirit inhibiting the magic of their foes over a wide area.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-14T18:39:44-07:00","id":1542155,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1516645-mage-game-styles?p=1542154#post1542154\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Or the Scourge existed, but it worked differently from how the books portray it--maybe various supernatural entities would either bolster or suppress certain kinds of sphere magic, and over time accounts of that have gotten garbled into what modern mages think the Scourge was (there's not <i>that</i> many mages who were around during that period). That's still not Paradox or the Consensus because the sources is very different, but it could be difficult for an external observer to tell the difference between a Reality Zone generated by Consensus and a local god or powerful spirit inhibiting the magic of their foes over a wide area.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> You know what, that might be an explaination for the &quot;the consensus is not natural&quot; plot that you and Monteparnas are thinking about. <br /> <br /> If I remember correctly, in the bygone bestiary book, dragons are incarnations of powers. Things so powerful, that even in a world where bygones must survive in quintessence hidden on nodes, or in the umbra, dragons still sleeps under the earth, merely inconvenienced by the disbelief, and it is said that when they raised, they &quot;remind the world of those things they forgot&quot;. <br /> <br /> And more importantly, it mention one dragon, whose name I forgot. A being of science and technology living in a clockwork realm, and with interest toward the ascent order of reason. <br /> <br /> <br /> That could be an origin point for the consensus:humans shapes reality to a degree like the superstition mecja ics in dark ges, but it's the influence of powers like this dragons or powerful umbroods that empowered that principle to the degree we know. <br /> <br /> <br /> The ascension war may have a secret pendant amongs bygones and high umbrood.<br /> <br /> <br /> Fun fact, int he recent M20 story compendium, there's one where a recently awakened girl end up in shenanigans with a ascent godling. She fight the creator of this god, a virtual adept, who fears that he may try to control humanity. <br /> <br /> And the young god explain that &quot;mages manipulates humanity's beliefs for their own ends, but gods finds it even easier to manipulate mages' beliefs&quot;. <br /> <br /> <br /> Add to that the fear of virtual adepts toward super AI in the tradition books of M20, and you have a way to escape the problem of the consensus, we simply have to make the war for reality a fight between mages AND other powers. <br /> <br /> Which would match the themes of the game. After all is a fight of beliefs and philosophies. And many philosophies are based on obeying gods and spirits.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24043-nyremne","name":"Nyremne","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-15T20:51:09-07:00","id":1542218,"title":"","content":"Within the history of the setting, the Consensus being possibly unnatural isn't something new, if we take into account how Paradox works.<br /> <br /> At first, no force countered Awakened magic whatsoever. We know this because the rise of new, unforeseen limitations on Magic is a plot point for the dawn of the Dark Ages and the formation of the Order of Hermes. It is also the whole point behind House Tremere experimenting with vampiric blood, and eventually turning into vampires.<br /> <br /> Even then, at first the only thing that Mages have to be worried about is Backlash, a problem arising only when they botch on spellcasting, which is how things work in Mage Dark Ages. Only by the time of Sorcerer's Crusade, already at the beginning of the Renaissance, does we have actual talkings about a Consensus, and the Scourge appears. Even then, it works very differently still from Paradox. By then, it is still defined mostly locally, and a global Consensus is more theory than anything.<br /> <br /> Paradox as we know it has a precise date of birth: 14 June, 1645, at the Battle of Naseby. The theory goes that it was the result of the Scourge changing under the pressure of too much vulgar magic. And even then, the strength of Paradox changes over time, reaching its apex at the end of the 90's.<br /> <br /> That's just to show that, as far as metaphysics goes, the setting doesn't really relies on the Consensus being a natural or ever-present phenomenon. We know that people's beliefs shape reality in some way, but a lot of our assumptions about how it work derive from something that hasn't always been there, and in fact that could be linked to beliefs only from the 15th century onwards.<br /> <br /> It becomes even more problematic when we start to examine how many things go unexplained by the Consensus. It is blatantly clear that a fundament for reality exists, as a lot of things are real despite general belief, and that goes from monsters to diseases. In fact, it is even more clear given that the Technocracy's influence over the population was greatly overrated. People not only still believe in magic, they still do so in droves and even in the most secular environments.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyremne</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1516645-mage-game-styles?p=1542144#post1542144\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">if we were to built a world where the consensus is an aberration, how can it be replaced without making it look like a massive retcon, so to speak?</div> </div> </div> </div>My idea, at least, isn't to replace the Consensus (at least not at first), but to have it weakening in much the same sense it has strengthened before. The idea isn't to retroactively replace the Consensus with something else, but to play its inconsistencies and, at first, to simply recognize the whole Revised Era rules for Paradox while dialing back to 2nd Edition Paradox.<br /> <br /> So, while it is maybe a bold move lorewise, as far as mechanics and storytelling goes, it's actually quite simple. I may come to use even less strict Paradox rules at some point, but that's about it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-16T06:43:11-07:00","id":1542245,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1516645-mage-game-styles?p=1542081#post1542081\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">2. We know there are alternate routes to magic and expanding the human technological base. It's less versatile and less powerful, but sorcerers can create new paths without any involvement from mages or any Mythic Threads (see the Starlight path, which has no real world folkloric basis). Yet mage factions are almost exclusively focused on Consensus and Sphere magic. There's a lot of other things that don't seem to be affected by Consensus and some logical issues posed by its existence that would be neatly explained if it didn't always exist and wasn't always as strong.</div> </div> </div> </div>This could be a really fun avenue to explore in the &quot;reality is breaking down&quot; or &quot;consensus is shifting&quot; storyline, I think. MRev and M20 were already gesturing vaguely in that direction by indicating that some in Traditions and the Technocracy as a whole that Sorcerers (or their equivalents) are at parity or even outnumber &quot;True Magic&quot; wielders.<br /> <br /> So instead of (or even alongside) balkanized reality zone neighborhoods you get a sudden explosion in sorcerers across all walks of life. Not just jewish mysticism in Midwood and voudu in Flatbush but even more unexpected places. An EMT lays on hands to stop the bleeding on a accident and finds himself using the path of Healing. The bored bartender unknowing working Herbalism or Alchemy. A street performer with Facination. A cab driver makes his usual morning prayer to Ganesha, remover of obstacles and suddenly finds himself working the path of Conveyance.<br /> <br /> (insert your own local reality zones and sorcerers here. I'm Brooklyn NYC based and am writing what I know).<br /> <br /> The themes such a story point would serve would be a counter to magical hubris. A reminder that the &quot;little people&quot; matter. In fact, right now they are where the real action is. And, perhaps, that is where the real action always was.<br /> <br /> Plotwise, there are a variety of reasons this could be happening. One of the more hopeful interpretations would be that a distracted (threat null) or reformed (post civil war) technocracy has loosened the reins on enforcing conformity and therefore the consensus. Heterodox belief flourishes not only within communities but within individuals as well. And again, perhaps it was always this way. Most people muddle through their day with a mixture of faith in a higher power and a belief that science will ensure their car turns on and their aspirin works the way its supposed to. Very few people in the modern world actually are creationists or anti-theists. All it took was the Technocracy to take its foot of the gas pedal in the form of the Pogram and the Timetable.<br /> <br /> Perhaps it was never a &quot;Consensus of Apathy&quot; at all. Maybe it was just the birth pains of a &quot;Consensus of Pluralism&quot; being born.<br /> <br /> Or there is a Nephandic weakening of reality causing this. The new sorcerers are a symptom of this problem, not the cause. Nevertheless they could be targeted by factions who do not realize this and think by reducing the number of sorcerers they can slow or reverse the deterioration of reality. Or we could use this to explore the &quot;evil always plants the seeds of its own demise&quot; trope as sorcerers are able to band together and strike back at the Nepahndi. These practitioners, ignored and belittled in the greater Ascencion War may be able to win a victory on par or greater than the victory won by the Council and Union combined at the end or WWII.<br /> <br /> In any of these scenarios there will be a lot going on as all factions maneuver in response to this sudden flourishing. A goldrush or a bloodbath depending on where you stand. Each one has a target on their back to be killed, convinced, suborned, recruited, or abducted. Even the best intentions can seem horribly disruptive to a normal person.<br /> <br /> Take our EMT suddenly working the Path of Healing. The Progentior's note her technical expertise and level head in stressful situations. They want her for field work. The Celestial Chorus notes she was raised Catholic, still prays to St. Michael, and her abuela was a consor for a Chorister during the 70's. They want her in a Chantry as a medic and caregiver for those on the violent fronts of the Ascension War.<br /> <br /> What does she want? She wants a good union job with decent benefits. She wants her partner, their kid, her abuela and her whole extended family and friend group not to to be at risk of being murdered by any number of the vast ancient magical conspiracies that are now SEVERELY complicating her life.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6350-marcusvitel","name":"MarcusVitel","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"20"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}